      "text": "{\n  \"first_impression\": \"At first glance it looks like another minimalist low-poly cozy game, but the actual mechanics have teeth. Bringing in real sheepdog trial rules, stubborn rams, mother dogs, and wolf stealth turns what could have been a generic walking simulator into a legit arcade tactics game. For a $10 indie, the atmosphere and level gimmicks look surprisingly tight.\",\n  \"scores\": {\n    \"visual_appeal\": 7,\n    \"readability\": 7,\n    \"game_feel\": 6,\n    \"fun_factor\": 7,\n    \"variety\": 8,\n    \"sense_of_progression\": 8,\n    \"want_to_keep_playing\": 7,\n    \"would_wishlist\": 8\n  },\n  \"best_things\": [\n    \"The variety of level hooks - from formal sheepdog trial gates to night-watch wolf defense and weather hazards - keeps the core herding mechanic from going stale.\",\n    \"The charm loadout system and Old Fly companion ability add genuine tactical depth and replayability beyond just chasing a three-star rating.\",\n    \"The color palette and cozy Scottish highlands storybook presentation make sunset pasture work feel genuinely atmospheric.\"\n  ],\n  \"worst_problems\": [\n    {\n      \"problem\": \"The camera angle in third-person view creates severe blind spots behind riverbanks and obstacles, making it hard to track stray sheep.\",\n      \"where\": \"e1-low-view.jpg\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"suggestion\": \"Keep the camera locked to a consistent high isometric angle with zoom controls rather than dropping low behind the dog.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"problem\": \"The storm rain particle effect is an aggressive mess of white slashes that obscures ground visibility and makes tracking sheep difficult.\",\n      \"where\": \"evening-7-storm.jpg\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"suggestion\": \"Tone down the rain line opacity, soften the streaks, and add subtle puddle ripples instead of screen-filling scratch marks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"problem\": \"Sheep cluster into a single fused polygon mesh with zero separation, making it impossible to count individuals or tell which ewe is stuck.\",\n      \"where\": \"e1-first-home.jpg\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"suggestion\": \"Add gentle soft-body separation or silhouette outlines to sheep so tight flocks do not become an unreadable white blob.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"problem\": \"Crucial HUD information is scattered across all four corners, forcing constant eye darting during fast-paced wolf alerts.\",\n      \"where\": \"e1-wolf.jpg\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"suggestion\": \"Consolidate the flock tally and wolf timer into a cleaner top-center cluster closer to the main play area.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"problem\": \"The border collie model and animations look rigid and taxidermied, lacking the low, fluid stalking gait real herding dogs are known for.\",\n      \"where\": \"evening-4-angus.jpg\",\n      \"severity\": \"cosmetic\",\n      \"suggestion\": \"Improve the sneaking and running animations with more torso flex and head-down stalking posture to sell the collie fantasy.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"boredom_points\": [\n    \"The 3+ minute countdown on Night Watch (evening-5-night.jpg) waiting out the clock around a stationary pen if the herd is already secured.\",\n    \"Slowly inching stubborn sheep across narrow footbridges when physics or pathfinding snag on fence geometry.\"\n  ],\n  \"confusions\": [\n    \"How the Z and X flanking controls behave relative to camera rotation compared to standard directional steering.\",\n    \"Whether 'Wolf Moon' mode is an optional difficulty toggle for completed levels or a separate unlocked campaign path.\"\n  ],\n  \"wish_list\": [\n    \"Full controller support with analog stick pressure sensitivity for stalking versus trotting.\",\n    \"A free-play sandbox pasture mode where you can herd without timers or wolf attacks.\",\n    \"More working dog breeds or cosmetic coats for Pip to unlock with ribbons.\"\n  ],\n  \"one_line_review\": \"Part cozy pastoral daydream, part tense tactical arcade game, Woolgather turns real sheepdog trials into something surprisingly smart and addictive.\"\n}",
